# One reader at a time per worker pipe

An RPC reply and unsolicited events (preview, health, `SegmentSealed`) arrive on
the same pipe and share one `FrameDecoder`. `stash_unsolicited` silently drops
message types it does not recognise, so if the event drain reads a `StopReply`
first, the RPC caller never sees it and blocks for its entire timeout. That
presented as `Stop` taking exactly 120 s while the worker had finished in 2.6 s.

Every pipe read must hold `SpawnedWorker::io_mutex`:

- `worker_rpc` takes it with `std::lock_guard` for the whole write-then-read.
- `drain_events` takes it with `std::try_to_lock` and returns 0 when busy,
  because preview is best-effort and must not block a control RPC.

Do not add a third reader. If you need one, route it through `drain_events`
rather than calling `read_frame` directly.

Keep the lock per worker, not global: multi-camera Stop runs in parallel and a
shared lock would serialise it.
